    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass (the suite's only test class is `AcceptanceTest`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` rule following the codebase's established pattern of small, self-contained `ResponseBodyAdvice` units.\n\n**Changes (2 files):**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` (boolean, readOnly) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets a `getRiskFlag`/`setRiskFlag` accessor, exactly like the sibling derived fields (`possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`).\n\n2. **`OwnerRiskFlagAdvice.java`** (new) \u2014 a small advice that sets `riskFlag = possibleDuplicate OR city-over-soft-capacity OR disposable-adjacent-email`. It **delegates** the duplicate and capacity computations to the existing `OwnerPossibleDuplicateAdvice` and `OwnerCapacityWarningAdvice` beans (invoking their `beforeBodyWrite` to populate the DTO, then reading the results), so it duplicates none of that logic and requires no edits to those classes. Only the disposable-adjacent check is new: an email's domain is flagged when any of its dot-separated labels matches a blocked disposable label (`mailinator`/`tempmail`/`guerrillamail`) \u2014 i.e. a different TLD or a subdomain of a blocked disposable domain, which is broader than the exact-match block (those are already rejected at request time) and hence the meaningful \"adjacent\" case.\n\n**Structural-impact notes:** I touched no existing method (WMC_other of every changed function is ~0 \u2014 everything lives in one new, small class), added no complexity to any large class, and delegating rather than recomputing kept the new methods low-CC. The only edits are one additive schema block and one new cohesive file.",
